      "template": "The gathering is {1} and the venue is {2}, with expected attendance of {3}. Its duration is {4}, its time frame is {5}, and the audience is {6}.\nThe weather is {7} and access to the surrounding area is {8}. The picture regarding medical incidents is {9}; visitor communications are {10}, while visitor flows are characterised by {11}.\nPermitted capacity is {12} and admission is {13}. Medical preparedness consists of {14}; decisions are coordinated through {15}, visitors receive information through {16}, and {17} is arranged in case of interruption.\nFor visitors, the health effects are {18}. The effect on the programme is {19}; for local residents this means {20}, and public information needs are {21}. Afterwards, {22} follows.",
      "prompt": "Write a credible event-safety exercise scenario in English using all selected values. Structure the narrative around the lead-up, disruption, measures and effects. Describe visitor flows, cooperation among the organiser, municipality and emergency services, and a dilemma involving access, capacity, information or weather. Distinguish expected attendance from permitted capacity: exceeding capacity is a potential problem, not an impossible combination. Make assumptions explicit; do not provide operational vulnerabilities or instructions for bypassing safeguards. End with two reflection questions.",
      "example": {
        "label": "A festival evening takes an unexpected turn",
        "desc": "<p>A cultural festival at an enclosed site expects several thousand visitors for an evening programme. Heat and delayed access cause a sudden surge of arrivals at the entrance. A few visitors need minor medical attention. The organiser temporarily pauses the programme and informs the audience over the public-address system.</p><p>At a joint meeting, the municipality and emergency services consider how permitted capacity, shelter in the surrounding area and access for local residents affect one another. The following morning brings aftercare and an evaluation of the decision and the communication.</p>",
