      "template": "The affected service is {1}, with a network connection of {2} and a critical dependency on {3}. The initial effects are felt by {4}.\nThe observable effect is {5}. The response is at the stage of {6}; the first signal is {7} and certainty about the cause is {8}.\nThe channel for the service is {9}, while digital availability is {10}. The temporary procedure is {11}; the status of the data is {12}.\nDecisions are led by {13}, and coordination with partners takes the form of {14}. Communication to users is {15}; the recovery status is {16}.",
      "prompt": "Create an English-language defensive cyber-continuity exercise scenario based on all selected values. Organise it around the first signal, verification, consequences for users, governance and recovery. Bring out trade-offs concerning uncertainty, data quality, communication and partner organisations. A service that is down digitally may continue through a temporary contingency process; do not confuse the service with its underlying technology. Distinguish observations from suspicions; do not describe attack steps, vulnerabilities or ways around safeguards. End with two questions about service delivery and accountability.",
      "example": {
        "label": "The municipal portal goes offline",
        "desc": "<p>Users report that a municipal online portal is no longer working. The organisation investigates with its supplier whether the existing data can still be trusted. A manual contingency process keeps priority requests possible, but requires additional explanation for residents.</p><p>The organisation-wide crisis team discusses with partner organisations how to share their situational picture. Digital recovery has not yet begun, so the initial public notice explains the effects on service delivery without naming an unconfirmed cause.</p>",
        "choices": [
